  "summary": "An architectural style reviving elements of Byzantine architecture from the Eastern Roman Empire.",
  "description": "Characterized by domes, mosaics, and rounded arches, it emphasized religious symbolism and opulence. This style emerged in Eastern Europe following independence from Ottoman rule, as a way to reclaim Orthodox Christian heritage.",
  "culturalContext": "Byzantine Revival — 19th-20th century architecture re-adopting the dome-and-gold program — built national-identity landmarks: London's Westminster Cathedral (1895-1903, red-brick Byzantine stripes), Neuschwanstein-era Germany's evangelical \"Byzantine\" churches, Greece's Athens Cathedral, and America's Greek Orthodox cathedrals. Alexandrian-Palace and Serbian Morava school revivals tied the style to Orthodox nationhood; the Masonic and ecclesiastical domes still anchor city skylines from Marseille to Melbourne.",
